1. **Unadjusted:** Black < White in all four quartiles; the gap narrows monotonically (RR 0.25 → 0.31 → 0.44 → 0.83) and
   reaches statistical *parity* — never a raw advantage — only in the top quartile (Fisher p = 0.72).
2. **Adjusted** (ASVAB + class percentile + sex + family size + Hispanic; g-computation, 1,000× race×SES-stratified
   bootstrap): Black > White everywhere above the bottom quartile and rising with SES (adj Black OR by quartile 0.99,
   1.66, 1.79, 2.16). Point-maximum in the top quartile (+3.05 pp, RR 1.91; boot CI −0.87 to +7.53, p(RD≤0)=0.065);
   the adequately supported pooled upper-half contrast is +2.40 pp (95% CI −0.03 to +4.99), RR 1.78 (0.99–2.65).
3. **Continuous curve:** adjusted RD rises monotonically, crosses 0 near SES ≈ −1.1 and its bootstrap CI excludes 0 over
   **SES +0.25 to +1.25 (≈ 68th–94th percentile; +1.0 alone is the 91st)**, reaching +2.8 to +3.4 pp there; beyond SES +1.5 support collapses
   (35 Black obs, 1 event) so the tail is direction-consistent but uninformative.
4. **Gradient:** Black completion is far more SES-dependent — OR per 1 SD of SES **2.07 (1.47–2.90)** vs White
   **1.17 (1.02–1.36)**; slope difference 0.565 ± 0.189, z = 2.99, **p = 0.003** (disjoint samples). Rates rise
   0.44%→3.03% (6.9×) for Black vs 1.78%→3.67% (2.1×) for White from Q1 to Q4. Adjusted, the gradient difference
   attenuates (0.257 ± 0.228, p = 0.26), as does the adjusted Black×SES(z) interaction (0.271 ± 0.201).
5. **Independent / form-free checks of the upper-SES advantage:** direct within-region adjusted logistic
   (statsmodels): upper half OR **1.88 (1.05–3.34), p = 0.033**; top quartile OR 1.93 (0.87–4.28); Firth 1.91 and 2.01.
   Mantel–Haenszel stratified on ASVAB quintiles: upper-half-SES OR **1.82 (1.02–3.26)**; top-quartile 1.91 (0.85–4.27);
   bottom-half-SES 0.86 (0.44–1.66) → the advantage is specific to the upper SES half.
6. **Firth (Jeffreys-penalised) sensitivity** — bias-reduced fit, appropriate for 8–16 events per contrast — confirms
   and slightly amplifies the pattern: adjusted RD **+2.64 pp (RR 1.84)** pooled upper half, **+3.32 pp (RR 1.97)** top
   quartile, +0.89 pp (RR 1.45) bottom half, +0.11 pp (RR 1.10) bottom quartile.

## Which framing holds
* **(a) Adjusted advantage — YES**, and it is largest at the top (top quartile / upper half).
* **(b) Parity or ratio-to-1 unadjusted — only approached**, at the top quartile (RR 0.83, CI includes 1) and numerically
  at SES > +1.0 (RR 1.02, 4 events). No stratum shows a statistically significant raw Black advantage.
* **(c) Steeper Black SES gradient — YES, the strongest single signal** (p = 0.003 unadjusted).

## Caveats
Raw Black rates are below White at every SES level; the advantage is entirely a same-resources comparison and it depends
on conditioning on the ASVAB composite (dropping ASVAB shrinks the top-quartile adjusted RD from +3.05 to +1.30 pp).
Only 28 Black completions exist overall (3–11 per quartile; 8 in the top quartile), so single-stratum CIs are wide and
the top-quartile result is *not* individually significant — the claim rests on the pooled upper half and on the
monotonic ordering across strata. Relaxing equal covariate slopes attenuates it (upper half +1.6 to +1.9 pp; top
quartile +2.3 to +2.5 pp); common-support restrictions (SES ≤ +1.5, ASVAB overlap) leave it positive (+2.2 to +2.6 pp).
Hispanic students behave like Black students (rate 0.75%→3.74% across quartiles; model OR 1.29) and pooling them with
Whites changes little (upper half +2.33 pp). SES is a standardized composite measured at high-school graduation,
completion is untimed, no sampling weights are available, and the file is a complete-case subset of NLS (max CASE ID
12,583), so rates are not national rates.
